      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 8 & -5 & k\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((-6)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot ((-4)\\cdot (k)-(2)\\cdot (-5)))+((-6)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (k)-(2)\\cdot (8)))+((-2)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (-5)-(-4)\\cdot (8)))\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot (10-4k))+((-6)\\cdot (5k-16))+((-2)\\cdot (7))\\\\ &=(8k-20)+(96-30k)+(-14)\\\\ &=62-22k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62-22k&=0\\\\ -22k&=-62\\\\ k&=\\frac{31}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{31}{11}.$",
